<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Hohenlohe</h1><p class="st-meta">1153 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Öhringen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Franconian county on the Kocher and Jagst rivers whose many branches of the Hohenlohe family ruled until mediatisation in 1806.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weikersheim">Weikersheim</a>, later called Hohenlohe after a castle near Uffenheim, appear around 1153 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Staufer</a> ministerials. They acquired lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauber">Tauber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocher">Kocher</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagst">Jagst</a>, and in 1450 were recognised as counts of the Empire, building a territory centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohringen">Öhringen</a>, Weikersheim and Neuenstein.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hohenlohe was a rural land of farms, vineyards and small residence towns. The family split into numerous lines, divided between Lutheran Hohenlohe-Neuenstein and Catholic Hohenlohe-Waldenburg after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weikersheim">Weikersheim</a> castle, with its baroque garden, and Langenburg are among their residences. Several branches were raised to princes in the 18th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 the Hohenlohe principalities were mediatised and divided between Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The family remained prominent: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodwig-zu-hohenlohe-schillingsfurst">Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</a> served as German Chancellor from 1894 to 1900.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1153 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.65%;width:13.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1153 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Öhringen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kraft of Hohenlohe, Chlodwig zu Hohenlohe-Schillingsfürst</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism and Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>East Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hohenlohe, Principality of Hohenlohe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1480&amp;focus=county-of-hohenlohe" aria-label="County of Hohenlohe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 462 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Hohenlohe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5219 718L5229 694M5219 718L5212 730M5219 718L5237 715"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0M5237 715h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 718h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 718h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Öhringen. <a href="/atlas?year=1480&amp;focus=county-of-hohenlohe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
